# device-layer-0023.
# The person's contacts, read: CNContactStore on macOS and iOS, the ContactsContract provider on Android. A browser has
# no contact search (the Contact Picker shows a sheet and answers what the person ticks), so it answers `unavailable`.
#
#   find-contacts(query)   the contacts whose name holds `query`, ignoring case, one a line, each the name, a tab, and
#                          its phone numbers joined by `, ` (nothing after the tab when it has none), sorted by name.
#                          An empty query matches everyone. Or none (no contact matches), not-determined (ask first:
#                          request-permission contacts), denied, unavailable or failed
#
# The match is made the same way on every host, by this module's own rule above, and never by a platform's search,
# because the platforms disagree: CNContact.predicateForContacts matches a name by its words' beginnings and the
# Android provider's LIKE anywhere in it. A name or number holding a tab or a line break has it read as a space.
#
# A host with no such capability (the server, the memory host) answers `unavailable`. Every answer is text from the
# closed set above, or the lines, so an app compares strings and no platform type ever reaches it.

load ./native/{platform}/contacts
  find search-contacts

task find-contacts
  take query, like text
  like text
  send back
    call search-contacts
      read query
